A federal election to determine the members of the 44th [[Parliament of Australia]] on 7 September 2013 is under way, although regular polling has finished. The [[Australian Labor Party|Labor Party]] was defeated by the [[Coalition (Australia)|Liberal/National Coalition]] on a current 14-seat 3.3 percent [[Two-party-preferred vote|two-party]] swing. The proclamation dissolving parliament and formally beginning the election period, was issued by the [[Governor-General of Australia|Governor-General]], [[Quentin Bryce]], on 5 August 2013. The [[Writ of election|writs of election]] were subsequently issued by Bryce and the state governors.

Voting in Australia's federal elections has been [[compulsory voting|compulsory]] since [[Australian federal election, 1925|1925]]. For the [[Australian House of Representatives|House of Representatives]], a [[instant-runoff voting|preferential ballot]] system has been in use since [[Australian federal election, 1919|1919]], in [[Single-winner voting system|single-member seats]]. For the [[Australian Senate|Senate]] – the [[proportional representation|proportionally representative]] upper house – a [[single transferable vote]] system has been in use since [[Australian federal election, 1949|1949]], with optional [[group voting ticket]]s since [[Australian federal election, 1984|1984]]. The election is conducted by the [[Australian Electoral Commission]] (AEC).

On 30 January 2013, Prime Minister [[Julia Gillard]] announced the election would be held on 14 September. However, following a [[Australian Labor Party leadership spill, June 2013|leadership ballot in June 2013]], she was replaced as leader by Rudd, who then abandoned the originally planned date. A [[Australian constitutional referendum, 2013|referendum]] on amending the [[Constitution of Australia|constitution]] to allow the [[Government of Australia|federal government]] to directly fund local councils that was initially planned to be held on the same day as the federal election could not go ahead on the date announced by Rudd.<ref>[http://news.ninemsn.com.au/national/2013/08/04/18/22/local-council-referendum-put-on-ice Local council referendum put on ice] [[Nine Network|Channel 9]] News, 4 August 2013</ref> This is because Section 128 of the [[Constitution of Australia]] requires that a referendum must be submitted to electors between two and six months after its passage through Parliament. As early voting started on 20 August it could not be submitted then.

