{{see also|Senate results for the Australian federal election, 2010}}
Before the election, the 76-seat [[Australian Senate|Senate]] was made up of senators from the Coalition (34), Australian Labor Party (31), Australian Greens (9), [[Democratic Labor Party]] (1), and one independent senator, [[Nick Xenophon]]. The Greens held the sole [[Balance of power (parliament)|balance of power]]. Previously the Greens had held a shared balance of power with the [[Family First Party]] and Xenophon.

Of the 76 Senate seats, 40 are contested. This corresponds to half of each state's allocation as well as both senators from the two major [[States and territories of Australia|territories]]. Newly elected state senators will commence their terms on 1 July 2014 and the senators of the territories will begin their terms immediately after their elections.<ref>''The election of territory senators coincides with general elections for the House of Representatives, and their term expires and the new term begins on the day of the election (Commonwealth Electoral Act, s. 42).'' [http://www.aph.gov.au/binaries/senate/pubs/odgers/pdf/odgers.pdf Odgers' Australian Senate Practice] 12th edn (2008), p. 17, at aph.gov.au</ref>

