==Result commentary==
The Labor Party's overall two-party preferred vote was its lowest since the [[Australian federal election, 2004|2004 election]],<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.theglobeandmail.com/news/world/australias-labor-leader-concedes-election-defeat/article14176600/|title=Australia's Labor leader concedes election defeat|first1=Rob|last1=Taylor|first2=James|last2=Grubel|newspaper=The Globe and Mail|date=7 September 2013|accessdate=8 September 2013}}</ref> while their 33% primary vote was the worst in a hundred years.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.theaustralian.com.au/news/breaking-news/palmer-says-he-can-win-over-the-nation/story-fn3dxiwe-1226714547252|title=Labor fears of Qld wipe-out averted|first=Stephen|last=Johnson|newspaper=The Australian|agency=Australian Associated Press|date=8 September 2013|accessdate=8 September 2013}}</ref> Kevin Rudd announced his resignation as party leader and confirmed he would not run again in the subsequent leadership election.<ref name=BBC1>{{cite news|title=Australia election: Tony Abbott defeats Kevin Rudd|url=http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-asia-24000133|date=7 September 2013|accessdate=8 September 2013|publisher=BBC News}}</ref>

With [[Nova Peris]]'s victory in the [[Australian Senate|Senate]] election in the [[Northern Territory]], she became the first [[Aboriginal Australians|aboriginal]] woman to be elected to parliament.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.ctvnews.ca/politics/first-aboriginal-woman-wins-australian-parliament-seat-campaign-brought-racism-1.1445284|title=First aboriginal woman wins Australian parliament seat; campaign brought racism|first=Rod|last=McGuirk|publisher=CTV News|agency=Associated Press|date=8 September 2013|accessdate=8 September 2013}}</ref> [[WikiLeaks]] founder [[Julian Assange]] failed to be elected to the Senate after running in [[Victoria (Australia)|Victoria]], with his [[The Wikileaks Party|Wikileaks Party]] garnering 0.62% of the popular vote.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.theguardian.com/world/2013/sep/08/election-2013-assange-wikileaks-party|title=Julian Assange: WikiLeaks party will continue|newspaper=The Guardian|agency=Australian Associated Press|date=8 September 2013|accessdate=9 September 2013}}</ref>

Final results are not yet published.<ref>[http://www.abc.net.au/news/federal-election-2013/results/ 2013 election results: ABC]</ref><ref name=aec>[http://vtr.aec.gov.au/Default.htm 2013 election results: AEC]</ref> Seat numbers are based on leading candidates.<ref>[http://vtr.aec.gov.au/HouseCloseSeats-17496-NAT.htm 2013 election close seats: AEC]</ref>

===Reactions===
The Coalition had campaigned on a tough stance on asylum seekers who came to Australia by boat (as had the Labor Party in the final weeks leading up to the election). Immediately after the election, Abbott reiterated his party's promise and announced that his new government would begin [[Operation Sovereign Borders]] — which would turn back any vessels carrying asylum seekers — as soon as possible. He also confirmed he would abolish the [[Carbon pricing in Australia|carbon tax]] that was introduced by the [[Gillard Government]],<ref>{{cite news|title=Australia's PM-elect Abbott vows quick action on asylum|url=http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-asia-24005687|date=8 September 2013|accessdate=9 September 2013|publisher=BBC News}}</ref> as well as lower foreign aid by [[Australian dollar|A$]]4.5 billion.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/australasia/australian-election-victorious-tony-abbott-vows-to-cut-foreign-aid-and-tackle-immigration-8803642.html|title=Australian election: Victorious Tony Abbott vows to cut foreign aid and tackle immigration|first=Rob|last=Williams|newspaper=The Independent|date=8 September 2013|accessdate=9 September 2013}}</ref>

Abbott was congratulated by various world leaders, including American President [[Barack Obama]],<ref>http://www.smh.com.au/federal-politics/barack-obama-calls-to-congratulate-tony-abbott-and-discuss-syria-20130913-2to7o.html </ref> British Prime Minister [[David Cameron]],<ref name=BBC1/> New Zealand Prime Minister [[John Key]]<ref>http://www.stuff.co.nz/world/australia/9140853/Key-expects-strong-ties-with-Abbott</ref> and South African President [[Jacob Zuma]].<ref>[http://www.timeslive.co.za/politics/2013/09/10/zuma-congratulates-australia-s-abbott-on-pm-election]</ref>

